What this solution actually does: T24 is a request-driven payment engine, not a timer poller. Despachador sends a TCP packet containing operation descriptors and folios; the host creates a DI scope and invokes OperacionServicio (ServiceHost.cs:484-493 (../../General/T24/T24/ServiceHost.cs#L484-L493)). A 38-case switch routes each operation to its family handler (OperacionServicio.cs:181-346 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/OperacionServicio.cs#L181-L346)). The handler reads the pending phase from the work plan, obtains general data, submits to T24 and sometimes SPEI/SPID/TEF, then reconciles the external record before advancing the plan. Most T24 submissions use middleware/OFS transaction control; FXI and TMD also use generated SOAP clients. SER_PAY and TAX_PAY coordinate a T24 debit and BBVA CIE payment with TSEC/HSM credentials (SER_PAY.cs:632-721 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/Servicios/Operaciones/SER_PAY.cs#L632-L721), TMD.cs:482-491 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/Servicios/Operaciones/TMD.cs#L482-L491)). Signing, authorization, reconciliation and local status updates cross several systems, so a lost response is not equivalent to a declined payment. The shared TCP response is calculated from the Ok/Error counters and Despachador retries ERROR/PARCIAL packets (ContratoTCP.cs:71-83 (../../General/MUFGT24_BTMUM_64/MUFG_T24/Core/DTOs/ContratoTCP/ContratoTCP.cs#L71-L83), DespachadorWorker.cs:122-142 (../../General/Despachador/Aplicacion/Servicios/DespachadorWorker.cs#L122-L142)).
