// TMD.cs:771-773
request.OfsRequest = P["WST24_MW_MODULE_TMD"] + "," + P["WST24_MW_VERSION_TMD"] + "/A/PROCESS,"
                   + P["WST24_MW_USERNAME_AUT"] + "/" + P["WST24_MW_PASSWORD_AUT"] + "," + transactionId;
Config.EscribirLog($"INFO|{tipoOperacion}.autorizaLD|Se envia el OFS para autorizar: {request.OfsRequest}");
Issue / impact: The time-deposit flow creates the LD record with one user and authorizes it with a second user (maker/checker). The checker credentials are concatenated into the OFS command (USER/PASSWORD) and the whole command is logged at INFO for every authorization. The file logger only masks key=value pairs and a fixed list of keys, so USER/PASSWORD inside an OFS command is not masked, although the parameter is declared sensitive precisely so that it does not reach the logs. The same credential also travels in the SOAP body over the generated cleartext HTTP endpoint (T24-FR-9). A person who can read the logs can authorize T24 loan/deposit records as the checker, which breaks the segregation of duties.
Recommended change: Remove the log line or log the command with the credential replaced by a fixed token (build a separate logSafeOfs string). Rotate WST24_MW_PASSWORD_AUT and review who read the logs. Add a masking rule to the shared logger for the OFS credential shape (/<user>/<password>,) and a test that logs an OFS string and asserts the password is absent.
Benefit: The checker credential is no longer recoverable from the logs, and the maker/checker control keeps its meaning.
Suggested JIRA: T24-FR-2 (Blocker, MustChange/rewrite).
T24-FR-3 - Certificate validation is switched off for the whole process and SSL 3.0 / TLS 1.0 are enabled when the first BBVA call runs (Blocker)
Category: MustChange/rewrite Priority: Blocker
Evidence: BBVA_comun.cs:60-62 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/Servicios/Comunes/BBVA_comun.cs#L60-L62) (Generate_TSEC), BBVA_comun.cs:255-257 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/Servicios/Comunes/BBVA_comun.cs#L255-L257) (Payment_CIE), the callback BBVA_comun.cs:550-557 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/Servicios/Comunes/BBVA_comun.cs#L550-L557), and the competing process-wide assignment in the shared mail helper CorreoHelper.cs:74 (../../General/MUFGT24_BTMUM_64/MUFG_T24/Infraestructura/Correo/CorreoHelper.cs#L74).
Code block (csharp)
// BBVA_comun.cs:61-62 (repeated at L256-257)
ServicePointManager.SecurityProtocol = (SecurityProtocolType)3072 | SecurityProtocolType.Tls | SecurityProtocolType.Ssl3 | (SecurityProtocolType)3072;
ServicePointManager.ServerCertificateValidationCallback = new RemoteCertificateValidationCallback(AcceptAllCertifications);
// L550-557: AcceptAllCertifications(...) { return true; }
Issue / impact: ServicePointManager settings are static for the process. From the first BBVA call onward every HttpWebRequest in the service process accepts any server certificate (including an expired, self-signed or wrong-host one), and SSL 3.0 and TLS 1.0 are allowed. The calls protected by this are the BBVA token and CIE payment requests, which carry the TSEC token, the client-certificate session and the payment payload, so a man-in-the-middle on the path to the BBVA gateway is not detected. The shared mail helper assigns Tls12 to the same static property on every e-mail, so the effective protocol set depends on which call ran last, and the accept-all callback is expected to apply to the SMTP client as well (static state; not executed).
Recommended change: Remove the callback and the SSL 3.0/TLS 1.0 flags. Set the protocol once at start-up (TLS 1.2 or higher) and never per request. If the BBVA gateway needs a private CA, pin that CA or the server thumbprint in a callback set on the individual request (request.ServerCertificateValidationCallback), not on ServicePointManager. Remove the per-mail assignment in CorreoHelper in the shared-library row.
Benefit: Server identity is verified for the payment gateway and the protocol downgrade path is closed.
Suggested JIRA: T24-FR-3 (Blocker, MustChange/rewrite).
