// SER_PAY.cs:708-720 (TAX_PAY.cs:864-877 is the same) - ANY exception while sending
var request = await _serDao.ObtenerRequestBBVA(folio);
if (request == null || DameTexto(request.Stage) != "SUCC")
{
    await _serDao.ActualizarRequestBBVA(folio, "DECL", ex.Message);
    await _serDao.ActualizarTablaTrabajoEstatus(..., ConstantesEstatus.Recc);
}
return true;      // next phase: Stage == "DECL" -> T24_REGISTRO(folio, "RELEASE")
Issue / impact: The CIE payment is a two-system operation: T24 holds the debit and BBVA executes the payment. When the connection drops, the request times out or the response cannot be read after the request has left, the outcome at BBVA is unknown. The client stores DECL for that case, the orchestration records the operation as rejected, and the BBVA_AUTORIZACION phase then runs the T24 RELEASE that returns the customer's money while BBVA may already have paid the service. Each attempt also asks for a fresh ConsumerRequestID (T24-FR-48), so a manual retry does not reuse the idempotency key of the first request. Expected outcome: a customer debit released for a payment that was executed (static reading of the code and SQL; not executed against BBVA).
Recommended change: Add an explicit unknown state (for example UNKN) to the request table, set it for no-response, timeout and generic exceptions, and keep the T24 debit held. Resolve it with BBVA (an inquiry by ConsumerRequestID or CIE guide if the API offers one; otherwise a manual-review queue and one CRITICO alert). Persist the ConsumerRequestID before the call and reuse it for any retry of the same folio. Only a decline code returned by BBVA may move the folio to DECL and RELEASE. Add tests for timeout, lost response and exception-after-send asserting that no RELEASE is issued.
Benefit: A payment that BBVA executed can no longer be reversed in T24 by a network error, and duplicate payments caused by retries with a new key are avoided.
Suggested JIRA: T24-FR-5 (Blocker, MustChange/rewrite).
