Evidence: BBVA_comun.cs:66-67 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/Servicios/Comunes/BBVA_comun.cs#L66-L67) (Generate_TSEC) and BBVA_comun.cs:261-262 (../../General/T24/Aplicacion/Servicios/Comunes/BBVA_comun.cs#L261-L262) (Payment_CIE).
Code block (csharp)
X509Certificate2Collection certificates = new X509Certificate2Collection();
certificates.Import(rutaStore, passwordStore, X509KeyStorageFlags.MachineKeySet | X509KeyStorageFlags.PersistKeySet);
// no Dispose / Clear / Reset anywhere in the method
Issue / impact: A CIE payment performs two imports (token and payment). PersistKeySet asks Windows to keep the private key container in the machine store after the certificate object is gone, and the collection is never disposed, so each import is expected to leave a key container file and a live handle behind. The service therefore accumulates private-key material on disk (outside the HSM) and handles for as long as the process runs. Windows behavior; not measured here.
Recommended change: Load the certificate once (singleton created at start-up) with X509KeyStorageFlags.EphemeralKeySet where the platform supports it (or MachineKeySet without PersistKeySet), reuse it for every request, dispose it at shutdown, and read the PKCS#12 password through the HSM path only once.
Benefit: No key container is left on disk per payment, and the import cost disappears from the payment path.
Suggested JIRA: T24-FR-6 (Major, MustChange/rewrite).
