5. Architecture-Fit Note
T24 is a long-lived TCP listener executing resumable payment phases across database work plans and external gateways. Apply _ORCHESTRATION.md (./_ORCHESTRATION.md) section 6: fix correctness, credential protection and recovery before changing the hosting model. An in-place modernization is the initial recommendation; no demonstrated scaling or team-ownership requirement justifies a full microservice rewrite.
Option	Fit for T24	Decision / prerequisite
Modernize in place	Strong: existing per-folio phase model and dispatcher contract	Restore the build/test gate, type outcomes, centralize safe connectors and make phase markers observable first
Versioned message consumer	Plausible: packets already represent work requests	Requires durable delivery, deduplication, retry/dead-letter ownership and a contract replacing current ERROR/PARCIAL retry semantics
Explicit workflow/outbox	Strongest candidate for BBVA debit/send/release and SOAP LD create/authorize	First define unknown outcomes, reserve transaction identities and make each external/local step idempotent; evaluate a workflow engine only after operational requirements are known
Serverless	Weak without further evidence	Native HSM/WCF/network access, long reconciliation waits and service dependencies need validation against time limits and deployment constraints
Full microservices rewrite	Not justified by this audit	Requires a separate business case, SLAs, volumes, ownership and budget

Sequence: remove credential logs/persistence and TLS bypass; preserve ambiguous external outcomes; correct reconciliation/data/state defects; fix dependency restoration and add golden payload/SQL contract tests; then consolidate the handler registry, OFS and signature encoders. Keep the team's legacy-fidelity and SPEI-factory requirements. Unknowns include daily volume, packet size/latency, bank retry guarantees, database unique constraints and triggers, recovery objectives, release process and who owns shared MUFG_T24 fixes.
